<Layout
  title={"Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere: Where Exact Replay Ends and Tolerance Begins"}
  description={"About: Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere. Executed GE-H1 (100,000 ticks across Rust, Wasm32, C++20, Node BigInt, and Python: 0/1,000 SHA-256 mismatches) and GE-H2 (25,000-tick floating-point divergence horizons), while cross-GPU wgpu rendering tolerance remains open."}
>
  <section class="px-6 md:px-10 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="section-label mb-4">Research study · about</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-white tracking-tight">
      Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere: Where Exact Replay Ends and Tolerance Begins
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-body text-base text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
      This study asks whether a game can be written once as a checked state machine and then projected into several engines and languages. Both the surveyed specifications and two executed falsifier suites (Section 6.1) confirm the boundary: yes for game rules and simulation under pinned arithmetic (<code>GE-H1</code>: <code>0 / 1,000</code> pairwise <code>SHA-256</code> checkpoint mismatches across <code>100,000</code> ticks in native Rust, Rust WebAssembly, C++20, Node.js <code>BigInt</code>, and Python <code>3.13</code>); no when unpinned floating-point operations enter chaotic collisions (<code>GE-H2</code>) or when pixels are rendered across graphics hardware, where cross-GPU <code>wgpu</code> tolerance calibration remains open (<code>measured_j = None</code>, <code>board_metered = false</code>).
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">In plain words</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>Under <code>Q32.32</code> 64-bit integer physics (<code>GE-H1</code>), a 16-body 2D arena replays bit-for-bit across all five projections for <code>100,000</code> ticks (<code>0 / 1,000</code> <code>SHA-256</code> mismatches), spending <code>0.103 J</code> in native Rust, <code>0.109 J</code> in C++20, <code>0.448 J</code> in WebAssembly, <code>4.40 J</code> in Node.js <code>BigInt</code>, and <code>13.60 J</code> in Python <code>3.13</code> (Apple M5 Max <code>L3</code> <code>reported_j</code>).</li>
        <li>Under floating-point physics (<code>GE-H2</code>, <code>25,000</code> ticks), compiler <code>-ffast-math</code> fused multiply-add contraction diverges at tick <code>192</code>, V8 <code>Math.sin</code> vs system <code>libm</code> diverges at tick <code>37</code>, and <code>f32</code> precision diverges at tick <code>1</code> — each cascading into macroscopic gameplay desynchronization within <code>1,370</code> to <code>10,093</code> ticks.</li>
        <li>Cross-ISA (<code>x86-64</code> vs <code>arm64</code>) verification and <code>GE-H3</code> onward — including cross-GPU <code>wgpu</code>/WGSL pixel-error tolerance and multi-engine emitters — remain open work.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

